Grounding done — here's what I found, plus one thing I want to flag before we lock in the spec.
topbrands.kz checks out as described: a pure pay-to-rank leaderboard. Businesses list themselves, highest bidder holds #1 until outbid, minimum 1,000₸ → current #1 costs 11,500₸. No competitor-targeting involved anywhere in it — the 40,000₸+ in 24 hours came entirely from businesses bidding against each other for rank. That's the FOMO mechanic worth replicating. (milliondollartoilet.com is JS-rendered and didn't return readable content to the fetch, but the format — pay to put your own ad in a novelty, donation-counter-driven page — is clear enough from your description and the genre to work with.)
The one piece I won't spec out as described: placing a competitor's business on the platform without their consent, in a deliberately weak/mocking spot, so they have to pay to escape it. That's not a legal gray area in Kazakhstan — Article 16 of the Law "On Competition" names discreditation and incorrect comparison with a competitor as unfair competition outright, and the Law "On Advertising" separately bans ads that discredit, humiliate, or ridicule a party not using the advertised product. Both statutes judge effect, not phrasing, so no ToS language routes around it — and practically, it's also the fastest way to get your Kaspi/acquiring merchant account frozen and a rival filing with AZRK (the antimonopoly regulator) in week one, which kills the hype loop before it compounds.
Good news: you don't need that mechanic. topbrands.kz's own numbers show the bidding-war dynamic works when it's paying customers competing against each other — that's what I'd build the ТЗ around, with the toilet site's novelty/counter presentation layered on top as branding, not as a way to rope in non-participants.
